    "lawCode": "Amd §3, Gen City L; amd §§88-a, 572, 581, 593, 616, 629, 654 & 680-c, Gen Muni L; amd §1120-c, Pub Auth L; amd §§298 & 310, RP L; amd Town L, generally; amd §§17-1703-a & 17-1708, Vil L",
    "lawSection": "General City Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7389\n\nSPONSOR: BROUK\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the general city law, the general municipal law, the\npublic authorities law, the real property law, the town law and the\nvillage law, in relation to replacing all instances of the words coun-\ncilman or councilmen with the words council member or council members\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis bill would replace all instances of the words or variations of the\nwords councilman or councilmen with the words council member or vari-\nation thereof in order to update antiquated and inaccurate language and\nto promote broader participation in local government.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nTo amend the general city law, the general municipal law, the public\nauthorities law, the real property law, the town law, and the village\nlaw, in relation to replacing all instances of the words or variations\nof the words councilman or councilmen with the words council member or\nvariation thereof.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nGender does not disqualify a person from running for public office, yet\nunder current state law, the labels for many local public offices still\npresume participation by one sex over another. The use of gendered\nlabels is antiquated and inaccurate and unnecessarily degrades half the\npool of potential public servants. Recognizing this fact, in recent\nyears, the legislature has altered the use of gendered terms for police\nofficers and firefighters. As more women run for public office and join\nthe ranks of government, the continuing use of terms such as \"council-\nman\", \"councilmen\", \"alderman\" and \"aldermen\" denies the presence and\ninfluence of women throughout all levels of government.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n",
